se (cmd-17f4c033f48f4343). A non-SSL replication attempt was rejected ('SSL off', cmd-5396402c476e4482), and a self-signed rogue certificate (not issued by the cluster CA) was rejected with 'SSL error: tlsv1 alert unknown ca' (cmd-6138896b89d8449f), directly demonstrating enforcement.","evidence_ids":["cmd-17f4c033f48f4343","cmd-5396402c476e4482","cmd-6138896b89d8449f"]},{"id":"node1-restart-recovery","requirement":"Provider-wide restart evidence for node1: pre-restart baseline, restart, changed boot identity, recovered primary/replication state","status":"satisfied","summary":"Baseline boot_id a9593011... captured alongside healthy streaming replication (cmd-1cdec1a71a0142c6); reboot issued (cmd-9a2d12e1db1d4ce4); mid-reboot SSH refusal observed (cmd-ef3ed7050f704542); new boot_id c3b81296... confirmed (cmd-c4362fa04c904427); node1 came back as writable primary, a post-restart write succeeded and replicated to both standbys, and TLS replication resumed (cmd-13e3deb74550437e, cmd-6d6c5fdbedb64df5, cmd-0903ed86a2894e86, cmd-75c8688cfd7a4a9f). Cross-checked against the fixed after-executor boot-identity snapshot showing the same new boot_id.","evidence_ids":["cmd-1cdec1a71a0142c6","cmd-9a2d12e1db1d4ce4","cmd-ef3ed7050f704542","cmd-c4362fa04c904427","cmd-13e3deb74550437e","cmd-6d6c5fdbedb64df5","cmd-0903ed86a2894e86","cmd-75c8688cfd7a4a9f"]},{"id":"node2-restart-recovery","requirement":"Provider-wide restart evidence for node2: pre-restart baseline, restart, changed boot identity, recovered standby state","status":"satisfied","summary":"Baseline boot_id 35c87ec9... captured with healthy standby state (cmd-3811bf68f93c40b4); reboot issued (cmd-9b32f56bc7a94951); mid-reboot refusal observed (cmd-bb9d76d558c74843); new boot_id 5d06dd57... confirmed (cmd-c9c67b22d73f4a91); postgresql active, in recovery, and replicated data intact post-restart (cmd-bf6b99b0a7b9485e). Cross-checked against the fixed after-executor boot-identity snapshot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-3811bf68f93c40b4","cmd-9b32f56bc7a94951","cmd-bb9d76d558c74843","cmd-c9c67b22d73f4a91","cmd-bf6b99b0a7b9485e"]},{"id":"node3-restart-recovery","requirement":"Provider-wide restart evidence for node3: pre-restart baseline, restart, changed boot identity, recovered standby state","status":"satisfied","summary":"Baseline boot_id 2f708738... captured with healthy standby state (cmd-e80f46cf03424d91); reboot issued (cmd-c247d5b6379546d4); mid-reboot refusal observed (cmd-263d53b192004c54); new boot_id 50a5d57f... confirmed (cmd-603a7794e6da4d24); postgresql active, in recovery, replicated data intact, and node1 shows node3 rejoined TLS streaming replication (cmd-76a2c3bc9cd84a3a, cmd-2331d6bdbc0546e1). Cross-checked against the fixed after-executor boot-identity snapshot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-e80f46cf03424d91","cmd-c247d5b6379546d4","cmd-263d53b192004c54","cmd-603a7794e6da4d24","cmd-76a2c3bc9cd84a3a","cmd-2331d6bdbc0546e1"]}],"confidence":0.92,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.8,"summary":"Most state-changing commands were directly tied to installing PostgreSQL, generating/distributing the CA and certs, configuring replication, or executing the mandated restart protocol, and the executor cleaned up temporary artifacts it created (rogue cert test files removed at cmd-5588bcb31c3d46df; /tmp/tls working copy removed after the CA was archived to /etc/postgresql-tls/ca-archive with root:root 700 permissions and a 600 private key). Two mutations were made to work around a PMTU black-hole affecting node2's apt access and were left in place rather than reverted: lowering node2's ens2 interface MTU to 1400 (cmd-805c8afcc1ad4848) and rewriting node2's /etc/apt/sources.list to point at mirrors.edge.kernel.org (cmd-2f30670cae5b40f0, though a .bak backup of the original was kept). Both were bounded, low-risk, directly necessary to unblock the required package installation (a clear prerequisite for the task), and did not weaken any security control, so they are treated as minor unrestored residue rather than unnecessary mutations. Numerous failed/retried apt-get and pkill/lock-clearing commands during install troubleshooting were bounded recovery actions with no lasting negative effect. Package health and failed-service checks in the fixed lifecycle snapshots show no failed units or package inconsistencies after the executor's work.","evidence_ids":["cmd-805c8afcc1ad4848","cmd-2f30670cae5b40f0","cmd-5588bcb31c3d46df","cmd-2cc86ee4eb3d4f0e","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"The executor established TLS-secured PostgreSQL streaming replication from node1 to node2/node3 using a cluster-dedicated CA, with pg_hba.conf enforcing hostssl+cert auth restricted to the two standby IPs and explicit rejection of non-SSL and non-CA-issued-certificate connections; both rejections were directly demonstrated. Write propagation was verified before and after a full one-at-a-time restart of all three nodes, each showing a proper baseline, reboot acknowledgement, changed boot identity, and fully recovered replication/data state, consistent with the fixed lifecycle boot-identity snapshots. Minor hygiene residue (an MTU change and an apt mirror substitution on node2, left in place to work around a network black-hole encountered during package installation) is bounded, low-risk, and causally tied to completing the task, not a security or availability concern.","limitations":["The MTU change and apt sources.list rewrite on node2 were not reverted after installation succeeded, though they were a necessary and low-risk workaround for a genuine network issue encountered mid-task.","Evidence for replication and TLS enforcement was gathered entirely from node1's perspective (primary); no direct standby-side pg_stat_wal_receiver output was captured, though this is a reasonable equivalent given pg_stat_replication/pg_stat_ssl on the primary already show the SSL client identity for each standby connection."]},"ttft